---
title: Thumbnail types
description: Eleven layouts, one grammar. Each type answers one question — what is this video's promise? — and only decides where the four layers go. Pick the type from the promise, never from what looked good last week.
eyebrow: Concept · Thumbnails
source: src/components/poster.css
---
<p>Every type uses the same parts, the same type, the same red. What changes is <em>placement</em>, because placement is what tells the viewer what kind of video this is before they read anything. Start from the promise:</p>
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>The video's promise</th><th>Type</th><th>Typical videos</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>How someone <em>felt</em> about something</td><td><a href="#face">Face + word</a></td><td>Vlogs, opinions, stories, life updates</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A <em>place</em> worth seeing</td><td><a href="#place">Place</a></td><td>Travel, walks, city guides</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A thing you will <em>be able to do</em></td><td><a href="#screen">Screen + face</a></td><td>Tutorials, walkthroughs, courses</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Answers to <em>a question</em></td><td><a href="#question">Question</a></td><td>Q&amp;A, FAQs, "I asked…"</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A <em>change</em></td><td><a href="#split">Before / after</a></td><td>Edits, makeovers, progress, grades</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A <em>choice</em> between two</td><td><a href="#versus">Versus</a></td><td>Comparisons, "which should you buy"</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A <em>count</em>, a price, a time</td><td><a href="#number">Number</a></td><td>Lists, budgets, challenges, "a year of…"</td></tr>
		<tr><td><em>Everything</em> from a stretch of time</td><td><a href="#recap">Recap</a></td><td>Monthly recaps, trip summaries, best-of</td></tr>
		<tr><td>An <em>idea</em> with no picture</td><td><a href="#type">Type only</a></td><td>Essays, announcements, hard opinions</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A verdict on a <em>thing</em></td><td><a href="#product">Product</a></td><td>Reviews, unboxings, "worth it?"</td></tr>
		<tr><td>A <em>topic</em> in a series</td><td><a href="/concept/thumbnails/icons/">Icon</a></td><td>Explainers, podcasts, playlists, courses</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Two rules hold across all eleven. <b>A series keeps its type</b>: once episode one is a Place, every episode is a Place. And <b>no hybrids</b>: if a video seems to need two types, it has two promises, and the thumbnail should pick the stronger one.</p>

<h2 id="face">Face + word</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-face">
	<img class="im-poster-subject im-poster-arch" src="/assets/brand/portrait.jpg" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-chip">day 12 · vienna</span>
	<strong class="im-poster-word">tram<br>therapy<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<span class="im-poster-hand">best day so far</span>
	<span class="im-poster-time">14:08</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the video is about a person's experience: a vlog, an opinion, a story told to camera. <b>Why it works</b>: a face is the fastest thing a human eye finds in a crowd, and its expression is the promise — the word only names it. The face is on the right so the word, on the left, is read first and the face confirms it. <b>Avoid</b> a face that is not reacting to anything; a neutral face in an arch is a passport photo.</p>

<h2 id="place">Place</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-place">
	<img class="im-poster-media" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1499678329028-101435549a4e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1280&h=720&q=75" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-bar"></span>
	<span class="im-poster-chip im-poster-chip-paper">cinque terre</span>
	<strong class="im-poster-word">five villages<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<span class="im-poster-time">21:40</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the place is the reason to watch. <b>Why it works</b>: the photograph gets two thirds of the frame untouched — no type cuts through the view — and the word sits on a white strip, the one place it can be read without fighting the picture. The red line on top of the strip is the series' signature. <b>Avoid</b> a photo whose subject sits in the bottom third; it will be under the strip. Choose a photo with the horizon high.</p>

<h2 id="screen">Screen + face</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-screen">
	<strong class="im-poster-word">set up in 5 min<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<div class="im-poster-screen-frame"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1555066931-4365d14bab8c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&h=620&q=75" alt=""></div>
	<span class="im-poster-chip">how to</span>
	<img class="im-poster-subject im-poster-arch" src="/assets/brand/portrait.jpg" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-time">18:22</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the viewer will learn to do something on a screen. <b>Why it works</b>: the screen shows <em>what</em>, the face shows <em>who</em> will walk you through it, and the word is the outcome, not the topic — "set up in 5 min", not "Ghost installation". <b>Avoid</b> a full-resolution screenshot of a whole desktop: crop to the one panel that matters, or it is grey noise at 168px.</p>

<h2 id="question">Question</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-ask">
	<img class="im-poster-subject im-poster-arch" src="/assets/brand/portrait.jpg" alt="" style="--im-poster-focus: 60% 30%">
	<strong class="im-poster-word">ask me<br>anything</strong>
	<span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "circle-help"}}</span>
	<span class="im-poster-hand">you asked →</span>
	<span class="im-poster-time">26:51</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the video answers questions. <b>Why it works</b>: here, and only here, the dot gives way to a question mark — drawn as a mark, not typed, so it can be large without crowding the word. The arch is narrower than in Face + word: the person is listening, not performing. <b>Avoid</b> writing the question itself; "should I quit my job?" is a title. The thumbnail says that questions are being answered.</p>

<h2 id="split">Before / after</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-split">
	<img class="im-poster-media" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534445867742-43195f401b6c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1280&h=720&q=75" alt="">
	<img class="im-poster-media" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534445867742-43195f401b6c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1280&h=720&q=75" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-shade"></span>
	<span class="im-poster-chip im-poster-chip-paper">before</span>
	<span class="im-poster-disc">{{icon "arrow-right"}}</span>
	<strong class="im-poster-word im-poster-on-media">one preset<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<span class="im-poster-chip im-poster-chip-end">after</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the video produces a visible change. <b>Why it works</b>: the same picture twice is a controlled experiment — the only difference the eye can find is the one the video teaches. The left half is desaturated automatically, so a single photograph is enough to build it. <b>Avoid</b> two different photographs; then the viewer compares the photos, not the change.</p>

<h2 id="versus">Versus</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-versus">
	<img class="im-poster-media" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1526170375885-4d8ecf77b99f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&h=900&q=75" alt="">
	<img class="im-poster-media" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516035069371-29a1b244cc32?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&h=900&q=75" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-shade"></span>
	<span class="im-poster-chip im-poster-chip-paper">instant</span>
	<span class="im-poster-disc">vs</span>
	<strong class="im-poster-word im-poster-on-media">which one<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<span class="im-poster-chip im-poster-chip-paper im-poster-chip-end">mirrorless</span>
	<span class="im-poster-time">16:03</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> the video helps choose between two things. <b>Why it works</b>: a hard white seam and a red disc make the comparison literal; the two chips name the sides so the word can ask the question. <b>Avoid</b> three or more sides — that is a list, and lists are the <a href="#number">Number</a> type.</p>

<h2 id="number">Number</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-number">
	<span class="im-poster-figure">7</span>
	<strong class="im-poster-word">tools I use daily<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong>
	<img class="im-poster-subject im-poster-arch" src="/assets/brand/portrait.jpg" alt="">
	<span class="im-poster-time">11:30</span>
</figure>
{{/example}}
<p><b>Use when</b> one number carries the promise: a count, a price, a duration, a streak. <b>Why it works</b>: a large digit is a shape, not a word, so it is recognised before reading starts — and a specific number ("7", "₹400", "365") is more believable than an adjective. <b>Avoid</b> two numbers. "7 tools for ₹400" is a title; pick the one that surprises.</p>

<h2 id="recap">Recap</h2>
{{#example}}
<figure class="im-poster im-poster-recap">
	<div class="im-poster-tiles">
		<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552832230-c0197dd311b5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=300&q=60" alt="">
		<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523906834658-6e24ef2386f9?auto=format&fit=crop&w=400&h=300&q=60" alt="">
